---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Food and recipes"
  description="The cooking system's name table. Most slots are simple, but ten of them hold arrays of variant names keyed by ingredient pairs."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Names & Words', href: `${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/` },
    { label: 'Food and recipes' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The cooking minigame has 192 named food items. The names live in
    <code>extracted/nds/data/ucc/names.ofs</code>. Most slots are
    simple — one slot, one name. But ten of them are
    <strong>multi-name slots</strong>: arrays of variant names keyed by
    the pair of ingredients the player used.
  </p>

  <h2>Simple slots</h2>
  <p>
    A simple slot is 68 bytes:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`+0x00  u32     header word (the variant key, or category code)
+0x04  60 bytes UTF-16-LE name + NUL terminator + padding`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The header word is a small integer telling the engine what
    category the slot belongs to. The rest is the name, encoded in
    UTF-16 with a NUL terminator and zero-padding.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-name slots</h2>
  <p>
    Ten of the 192 slots are larger than 68 bytes, and every one of
    them is a multiple of 68. Each is an array of sub-records, all
    sharing the same 68-byte layout. The base category is sub-record
    0; the recipe variants are sub-records 1 onward.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Slot</th><th>Sub-records</th><th>Base name (JP)</th><th>Variant family</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>57</td><td>2</td><td>ケーキ</td><td>ショートケーキ (Strawberry Shortcake)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>65</td><td>10</td><td>シチュー</td><td>クリームシチュー (Cream Stew)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>66</td><td>6</td><td>シチュー</td><td>ビーフシチュー (Beef Stew)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>101</td><td>2</td><td>アイス</td><td>バニラアイス (Vanilla Ice Cream)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>144</td><td>2</td><td>パン</td><td>あんぱん (Anpan)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>145</td><td>5</td><td>パン</td><td>チョコパン (Choco Bread)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>146</td><td>2</td><td>パン</td><td>クリームパン (Cream Bun)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>149</td><td>2</td><td>パン</td><td>ジャムパン (Jam Bun)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>151</td><td>9</td><td>パスタ</td><td>カルボナーラ (Carbonara)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>152</td><td>9</td><td>パスタ</td><td>ナポリタン (Napolitan)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The variant key</h2>
  <p>
    Each variant sub-record carries a 2-byte key in its header. The
    key is a pair of ASCII bytes — usually digits or symbols. For
    Cream Stew (slot 65, 10 sub-records), the keys are:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`'79'  '97'  '78'  '87'  '89'  '98'  '37'  '39'  '38'`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The "alphabet" here is <code>{3, 7, 8, 9}</code>, and the keys are
    the non-equal ordered pairs of those digits with a few specific
    combinations. The working theory: each digit stands for one
    ingredient slot, and the engine looks up the cooked dish by the
    pair of ingredients you used. So if you cooked with ingredients
    "7" and "9," it finds the record keyed <code>'79'</code> and uses
    its name.
  </p>

  <p>
    For carbonara (slot 151), the alphabet is
    <code>{'{(, *, 3, 5}'}</code> — a different per-recipe ingredient
    set. The same engine logic applies.
  </p>

  <p>
    We haven't fully confirmed the lookup logic by reading the
    engine code yet — that would require finding the cooking-minigame
    overlay and tracing its name-lookup routine — but the data fits
    the pattern very cleanly.
  </p>

  <h2>Why all variants of a recipe have the same Japanese name</h2>
  <p>
    For Cream Stew with 9 variant sub-records, every single sub-record
    has the identical Japanese string <code>クリームシチュー</code>.
    Only the 2-byte key differs. The game treats them as
    interchangeable for display purposes — what matters is the key,
    not the name.
  </p>

  <p>
    For translation that means we can safely give every variant of
    Cream Stew the same English name ("Cream Stew"), as long as we
    preserve the original key bytes exactly. The engine's
    ingredient-pair lookup still works because the key isn't part of
    the visible name.
  </p>

  <h2>Cross-reference with Game 1</h2>
  <p>
    Magician's Quest 1 (the English-released predecessor) doesn't
    have a cooking system. There's no canonical English for any of
    these recipe names. The translations we use come from dictionary
    forms ("Cake," "Stew") and from Japanese-food English-writing
    conventions ("Anpan" vs "Red Bean Bun" — a stylistic call we
    leaned toward "Anpan" since it's the spelling Wikipedia uses).
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
